# Code owners for the files that decide *what* CI checks.
#
# Statement integrity is the point: a change to a proof is reviewed by reading the proof, but a
# change to the gates, the toolchain pin, or the axiom allowlist can make every other check
# vacuous. Those files therefore require an explicit review from the repository owner.
#
# This file only assigns reviewers. It has no effect until "Require review from Code Owners" is
# enabled on the protected branch — see .github/BRANCH_PROTECTION.md.

# The gates themselves.
/.github/                       @thomasahle
/.github/workflows/             @thomasahle
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @thomasahle
/scripts/                       @thomasahle

# The build contract: which targets exist, which toolchain and which dependency revisions the
# kernel is run at. `lake-manifest.json` pins the exact Mathlib/CSLib commits.
/lakefile.toml                  @thomasahle
/lean-toolchain                 @thomasahle
/lake-manifest.json             @thomasahle

# The enforcing axiom audit. `AxiomAudit/Command.lean` defines `#assert_axioms` and holds the
# allowlist `{propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound}`; `AxiomAudit/Census.lean` and its two
# clients are the environment-level authority that no project module postulates anything.
# Widening any of them silently weakens every other theorem in the repository.
/AxiomAudit.lean                @thomasahle
/AxiomAudit/                    @thomasahle
/AxiomAuditCertificate.lean     @thomasahle
/AxiomAuditCertificate/         @thomasahle

# The file that states the current record is a statement-integrity file by definition.
/Frontier.lean                  @thomasahle
